Organization = {Assoc Comp Machinery; ACM SIGSAC; Univ Auckland},
Abstract = {Data privacy is becoming one of the most critical concerns in cloud
   computing. Several proposals based on Intel SGX such as VC3 {[}1] and
   M2R {[}2] have been introduced in the literature to protect data privacy
   during job execution in the cloud. However, a comprehensive formal proof
   of their security guarantees is still lacking. In this paper, we propose
   ObliDC, a general UC-secure SGX-based oblivious distributed computing
   framework. First, we model the life-cycle of a distributed computing job
   as data-flow graphs. Under the assumption of malicious, adaptive
   adversaries in the cloud, we then formally define data privacy of a
   distributed computing job by introducing a notion named ODC-privacy,
   which encompasses both semantic security (to protect data
   confidentiality during computation and transmission) and oblivious
   traffic (to prevent data leakage from traffic analysis). ObliDC is
   composed of four two-party protocols - job deployment, job
   initialization, job execution, and results return, which allow for
   modular construction of concrete privacy-preserving job protocols in
   different distributed computing frameworks. Finally, inspired by a
   formal abstraction for trusted processors proposed by R. Pass et al.
   {[}3], we formally prove the security of ObliDC under the universal
   composability (UC) framework.},
